  One rule written into that file as a comment, because it's the thing that will get violated later: [1m[38;5;153mgetUserId()[39m [0m
  [1mreturning a value is not an authentication check.[0m It says "this session previously completed a login," which is not
  the same as "this request is authorized." Authorization stays the server's job.

  [1m[38;5;153mlogin()[39m becomes async and gains a [38;5;153muserId[39m in its response:[0m

  [34masync[39m [34mfunction[39m [33mlogin[39m(username, password) {
    [32m// Stub: would POST to API_ENDPOINT and read userId from the response[39m
    [34mreturn[39m { [36msuccess[39m: [34mtrue[39m, [36muser[39m: username, [36muserId[39m: [31m`stub-[39m${username}` };
  }

  The stub value is prefixed [38;5;153mstub-[39m deliberately — so if it ever reaches a log or a backend, it's obviously not a real
  ID. The spec will record the expected real response shape as an assumption to validate against the actual endpoint.

  [1m[38;5;153mapp.js[39m becomes an ES module[0m, imports [38;5;153msetUserId[39m, and stores the ID after a successful login. [1m[38;5;153mindex.html[0m changes one
  line: [38;5;153m<script type="module" src="app.js"></script>[39m.

  [1mFlow on submit:[0m validate (unchanged) → [38;5;153mawait login(...)[39m → on [38;5;153msuccess[39m with a [38;5;153muserId[39m, [38;5;153msetUserId(result.userId)[39m → on
  failure, [38;5;153mclearUserId()[39m so a stale ID from an earlier login can't outlive it. Future forms do [38;5;153mimport { getUserId } from
[39m  [38;5;153m'./identity.js'[39m.

  [1mFiles touched:[0m [38;5;153midentity.js[39m (new), [38;5;153mapp.js[39m, [38;5;153mindex.html[39m. [38;5;153msrc/index.js[39m and [38;5;153msrc/utils.js[39m are an unrelated CommonJS pair not
  loaded by the browser — I'm leaving them alone.

